    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>Can a quantum computer crack bitcoin's private keys?</h2>
      <p class="qt-p">Bitcoin's ownership rests on <b>elliptic-curve cryptography</b> (secp256k1): your private key produces a public key, and reversing that math is out of reach for normal computers, because every computer on Earth working together would need longer than the age of the universe. A large, error-corrected quantum computer changes that one specific equation. <b>Shor's algorithm</b> could derive a private key from a <em>known public key</em> in hours to days. That is the whole threat. It is real, it is taken seriously, and it is also routinely exaggerated.</p>
      <p class="qt-p">Two limits up front. First, Shor's algorithm needs your <b>public key</b>, and most bitcoin is stored behind a <em>hash</em> of the public key, which quantum computers do not meaningfully break. Second, the other quantum algorithm people cite, <b>Grover's</b>, offers only a square-root speedup against hashing, far too weak to threaten SHA-256 mining or hashed addresses in practice. If the terms here are new, <a href="/learn/what-is-bitcoin">what bitcoin is</a> and the <a href="/learn/whitepaper">whitepaper guide</a> cover the ground underneath them.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>Which bitcoins are actually at risk?</h2>
      <p class="qt-p">Quantum exposure is not one property of "bitcoin." It depends entirely on whether an output's public key is already visible on-chain.</p>
      <div class="qt-scroll">
        <table class="qt-table" aria-label="Bitcoin output types and their quantum exposure">
          <thead><tr><th>Output type</th><th>Public key visible?</th><th>Exposure</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="qt-k">P2PK (2009 to 2010 era)</td>
              <td>Yes. Printed directly on-chain forever. Includes the roughly 1 million BTC attributed to <a href="/learn/who-is-satoshi">Satoshi's</a> miner.</td>
              <td><span class="qt-risk qt-hi">Exposed</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="qt-k">Reused addresses</td>
              <td>Yes. The first outgoing spend reveals the key, and every coin still sitting there is exposed.</td>
              <td><span class="qt-risk qt-hi">Exposed</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="qt-k">Taproot (P2TR)</td>
              <td>Yes. Taproot outputs carry a tweaked public key on-chain by design.</td>
              <td><span class="qt-risk qt-md">Exposed class</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="qt-k">Fresh legacy and SegWit (P2PKH, P2WPKH)</td>
              <td>No. Only a hash is on-chain until the moment you spend. The key is revealed only in the spending transaction's brief confirmation window.</td>
              <td><span class="qt-risk qt-lo">Protected until spend</span></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="qt-note">Roughly 4 million BTC, about a fifth of the supply, sits in the exposed categories today, mostly early P2PK coins and heavily reused addresses. You can see which output type an address uses with the <a href="/">address lookup</a>.</p>
      <p class="qt-p">This split matters. A future quantum attacker's easy targets are the old, exposed coins, which is exactly why the network would get a loud and unmistakable warning, dormant Satoshi-era coins suddenly moving, long before ordinary wallets were at risk. The exposed coins are the canary, and the canary is a decade of engineering time wide.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>When will quantum computers be able to break bitcoin?</h2>
      <p class="qt-p">The headline qubit counts you see in press releases are <b>physical</b> qubits: noisy, error-prone components. Breaking secp256k1 requires roughly <b>1,200 to 1,450 clean "logical" qubits</b> on Google Quantum AI's 2026 estimate (earlier estimates said about 2,300), and with error correction each logical qubit costs hundreds to thousands of physical ones. The most recent engineering estimates put a practical key-cracking machine at <b>hundreds of thousands of physical qubits</b>, down from earlier estimates in the millions. Today's best processors sit at low thousands of physical qubits, and the first few dozen logical qubits. The gap is not one breakthrough. It is still orders of magnitude of scaling, error correction, and control engineering, and the most recent estimates have made it smaller, not larger.</p>
      <p class="qt-p">The Global Risk Institute's 2025 expert survey puts roughly one-in-three odds on a "cryptographically relevant" quantum computer within a decade and calls one likely within fifteen years, with the heaviest expert weight on <b>2030 to 2035</b> and some respected researchers still saying much later or never. Progress is real, and error-correction milestones fall every year, which is precisely why Bitcoin's preparation started now rather than waiting.</p>
      <p class="qt-p">One more myth worth retiring. <b>"Harvest now, decrypt later"</b> is a genuine concern for encrypted messages, and it mostly does not apply here. Bitcoin's blockchain is not encrypted, it is public. What matters is whether your public key is visible, which the table above already covers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>What is the Bitcoin network doing about the quantum threat?</h2>
      <p class="qt-p"><b>The cryptography is ready.</b> In 2024 NIST finalized the first post-quantum signature standards, <code class="qt-code">ML-DSA</code> (Dilithium), <code class="qt-code">SLH-DSA</code> (SPHINCS+), and later <code class="qt-code">FN-DSA</code> (Falcon), after a decade-long global competition. Quantum-resistant signing is a solved problem. The open work is fitting it into Bitcoin well.</p>
      <p class="qt-p"><b>A bitcoin upgrade path is being designed in the open.</b> The most developed proposal is <code class="qt-code">BIP-360</code>, "Pay to Quantum-Resistant Hash" (P2QRH), a soft fork that would add a new output type letting coins be locked to post-quantum keys, with hybrid schemes so wallets can hold both classical and quantum-resistant keys during the transition. Hash-based one-time signatures (Lamport and Winternitz) are studied as a conservative fallback, since they rely only on hash functions Bitcoin already trusts.</p>
      <p class="qt-p"><b>The hard problems are trade-offs, not mysteries.</b> Post-quantum signatures are large. A Dilithium signature runs about 2.4&nbsp;KB against Schnorr's 64 bytes, so block space, <a href="/fees">fees</a>, and aggregation need real engineering. And the community must eventually answer an uncomfortable governance question: when the threat becomes concrete, what happens to exposed coins whose owners never migrate, including Satoshi's? Letting them be stolen rewards the attacker. Freezing them means confiscating someone's property by consensus. That debate, "burn versus steal," is already happening in public, years early, which is exactly where a debate like that belongs.</p>
      <p class="qt-p"><b>Migration, when it comes, will look boring.</b> A soft fork activates quantum-resistant outputs, wallets add a "send coins to your new quantum-safe address" button, and users move funds in an ordinary transaction over a period of years. Your seed phrase today is what authorizes that move tomorrow, which is why the practical advice below is so short.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>How do I protect my bitcoin today?</h2>
      <ul class="qt-checks">
        <li><b>Nothing urgent.</b> There is no action a normal holder needs to take today.</li>
        <li><b>Never reuse addresses.</b> This is already best practice for privacy, and it is also the quantum-exposure difference you control. Fresh address for every receive, as the <a href="/learn/privacy">privacy guide</a> explains.</li>
        <li><b>Keep your seed phrase safe.</b> Future migration is just a transaction, signed by the keys you hold now. Lost keys, not quantum computers, remain the realistic way bitcoin disappears. The <a href="/learn/self-custody">self-custody guide</a> covers storage, and <a href="/wallets">wallets</a> covers the software.</li>
        <li><b>Ignore "quantum-proof coin" marketing.</b> Projects selling quantum panic are selling something. Bitcoin's fix is an upgrade, not a different asset, and the pitch shows up in the same places as the <a href="/learn/scams">scam families</a>.</li>
        <li><b>Watch the BIPs, not the headlines.</b> The moment anything becomes actionable it will arrive as a reviewed soft-fork proposal, and this page will cover it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="qt-card qt-mt">
      <h2>Quick answers</h2>
      <details class="qt-qa">
        <summary>Could a quantum computer steal Satoshi's coins?</summary>
        <p>Eventually, in principle, yes. The roughly 1 million BTC mined in 2009 and 2010 sits in P2PK outputs with public keys fully exposed, and presumably no one holds the keys to move them first. That is why those coins are widely expected to be the first target and the network's early-warning system. Whether the community would let that theft happen, or freeze exposed outputs first, is the open "burn versus steal" debate.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="qt-qa">
        <summary>Would mining break?</summary>
        <p>No. Grover's algorithm offers only a quadratic speedup against SHA-256, and quantum gate speeds are slow next to ASICs performing quintillions of hashes per second. Even optimistic quantum mining is uncompetitive, and if that ever changed, the difficulty adjustment absorbs it like any other hardware advance. The <a href="/halving">halving page</a> covers how issuance and difficulty actually work.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="qt-qa">
        <summary>If my address only holds a hash, am I completely safe?</summary>
        <p>Until you spend, effectively yes. At spend time your public key becomes visible while the transaction confirms, a short race window that a very fast attacker could theoretically exploit. That scenario requires a machine far beyond even the key-cracking threshold, and proposed migrations, plus commit-reveal spending schemes, close it. It is an argument for finishing the upgrade before such machines exist, which is the plan.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="qt-qa">
        <summary>Does this change the case for holding bitcoin?</summary>
        <p>Quantum computing threatens every system secured by today's public-key cryptography: banks, governments, TLS, all of it. Bitcoin is unusual in having its exposure mapped coin by coin in public, a standardized replacement ready, and an upgrade culture that has already shipped consensus changes. The honest risk here is real but distant, monitored, and fixable. Treat anyone claiming otherwise, in either direction, as selling something. Nothing on this page is investment advice; see the <a href="/terms">terms</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

