    </div>

    <div class="qt-about doc-prose">
      <p>The quantum question about bitcoin has a narrow, answerable shape: a large error-corrected quantum computer running Shor's algorithm could recover a private key from a public key that is already visible on-chain, which is why old P2PK outputs and reused addresses are the exposed set and fresh, unspent hashed addresses are not. Expert surveys now put roughly one-in-three odds on that machine arriving within a decade, standardized post-quantum signatures already exist, and a Bitcoin soft-fork path is being drafted in public. Until it activates, the useful habits are the ordinary ones: a fresh address every time, a seed phrase you can still read in ten years, and no attention paid to coins sold on quantum panic. Everything here is informational, not financial or security advice, under the site's <a href="/terms">terms</a>.</p>
    </div>
